From 4240ae728e7ce931c9be5049878fdfd4cc7e0d79 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oliver Lukschander <oliver.lukschander@golf.at>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 10:12:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] thunderbolt: apple: fix dpin_attempted latching after real
 unplug

The 0112 same-boot re-test infrastructure (register clear + latch
reset on a clean deactivate) never actually ran on real hardware: a
physical unplug clears acio->current_cable_info (and often nhi_pdev)
before the DCP-issued DEACTIVATE APCALL reaches
apple_usb4_right_dpin0_set_active(), tripping the existing early-return
guard with the function's default -ENODEV before ever calling
apple_dpin_handshake(). Confirmed directly: the dpin_mode_value=10
test's own deactivate logged exactly this -19 (-ENODEV), and the very
next activate attempt (mode_value=0) never produced any handshake log
line at all -- silently blocked by dpin_attempted, which that skipped
deactivate never got to reset.

DPIN0's registers are on-die SoC hardware, not torn down by cable
removal, so when this guard trips on a deactivate request and we
previously activated (dpin_base already mapped), fall through to the
same cleanup path instead of bailing out with our own state stuck.
Only bail immediately when there is truly nothing of ours to clean up
(activating with no cable/session, or deactivating with nothing ever
mapped).

This was a mock-test blind spot, not caught by
scripts/test-dpin-handshake.c: that harness only covers
apple_dpin_handshake() itself, not this wrapper's cable-state guards,
since the mock has no notion of current_cable_info at all.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/thunderbolt/apple.c | 21 +++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/apple.c b/drivers/thunderbolt/apple.c
index 016afb6..bea230f 100644
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/apple.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/apple.c
@@ -1852,8 +1852,25 @@ int apple_usb4_right_dpin0_set_active(bool active)
 		goto unlock_device;
 	}
 	if (!acio->current_cable_info || !acio->nhi_pdev ||
-	    acio->rc_res->start != 0xf01ac0000ULL)
-		goto unlock_cio;
+	    acio->rc_res->start != 0xf01ac0000ULL) {
+		/*
+		 * A real physical unplug clears current_cable_info (and often
+		 * nhi_pdev) before the DCP-issued DEACTIVATE APCALL reaches
+		 * us, so this trips on every ordinary disconnect, not just a
+		 * stale or unrelated call -- confirmed by seeing exactly this
+		 * -ENODEV on a real deactivate during the 0112 sweep, which
+		 * silently skipped both the MODE_A/MODE_B register clear and
+		 * the dpin_attempted reset below, permanently latching out
+		 * every activate for the rest of that boot. DPIN0's registers
+		 * are on-die SoC hardware, not torn down by cable removal, so
+		 * if we previously activated (dpin_base already mapped), fall
+		 * through to the same deactivate/cleanup path as normal
+		 * instead of leaving our own state stuck. Only bail out here
+		 * when there is truly nothing of ours to clean up.
+		 */
+		if (active || !acio->dpin_base)
+			goto unlock_cio;
+	}
 	if (active == acio->dpin_active) {
 		ret = 0;
 		goto unlock_cio;
-- 
